      "text": "Given that \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is a right triangle with \\( \\angle A = 90^\\circ \\), \\( AB = 16 \\), and \\( BC = 24 \\). We need to find \\( \\sin A \\).\n\nFirst, recall that in a right triangle, the sine of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. Since \\( \\angle A = 90^\\circ \\), the side opposite to \\( \\angle A \\) is the hypotenuse \\( BC \\), and the opposite side to \\( \\angle A \\) is \\( AB \\).\n\nThe length of the hypotenuse \\( BC \\) is given as 24. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite to } \\angle A}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\n\nNext, simplify the fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( \\sin A \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThe sequence $\\{a_n\\}$ is defined by $a_1=2$ and $a_{n+1}=3a_n-1$ for $n\\geq1$. Find the sum $S=\\sum_{k=1}^{46}a_k$.\n\nTo find the sum \\( S = \\sum_{k=1}^{46} a_k \\) for the sequence defined by \\( a_1 = 2 \\) and \\( a_{n+1} = 3a_n - 1 \\), we first need to determine the explicit form of the sequence.\n\nStarting with the initial term:\n\\[ a_1 = 2. \\]\nUsing the recurrence relation \\( a_{n+1} = 3a_n - 1 \\), we can compute the next terms:\n\\[ a_2 = 3a_1 - 1 = 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 = 5, \\]\n\\[ a_3 = 3a_2 - 1 = 3 \\cdot 5 - 1 = 14, \\]\n\\[ a_4 = 3a_3 - 1 = 3 \\cdot 14 - 1 = 41. \\]\nWe observe that the terms are increasing rapidly, but to find a pattern or a general formula, we can look at the recurrence relation in a different form. Rewrite the recurrence relation as:\n\\[ a_{n+1} - 1 = 3(a_n - 1). \\]\nLet \\( b_n = a_n - 1 \\). Then the recurrence relation becomes:\n\\[ b_{n+1} = 3b_n. \\]\nGiven \\( a_1 = 2 \\), we have \\( b_1 = a_1 - 1 = 1 \\). Therefore, the sequence \\( b_n \\) is a geometric sequence with the first term 1 and common ratio 3:\n\\[ b_n = 3^{n-1}. \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ a_n = b_n + 1 = 3^{n-1} + 1. \\]\nNow, we need to find the sum \\( S = \\sum_{k=1}^{46} a_k \\):\n\\[ S = \\sum_{k=1}^{46} (3^{k-1} + 1) = \\sum_{k=1}^{46} 3^{k-1} + \\sum_{k=1}^{46} 1. \\]\nThe first sum is a geometric series with the first term 1 and common ratio 3:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=1}^{46} 3^{k-1} = \\frac{3^{46} - 1}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3^{46} - 1}{2}. \\]\nThe second sum is simply the sum of 1 added 46 times:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=1}^{46} 1 = 46. \\]\nTherefore, the sum \\( S \\) is:\n\\[ S = \\frac{3^{46} - 1}{2} + 46. \\]\nCombining the terms, we get:\n\\[ S = \\frac{3^{46} - 1 + 92}{2} = \\frac{3^{46} + 91}{2}. \\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3^{46} + 91}{2}}. \\]\n\nAlex says he will spend less than 6 hours studying this week. So far, he has spent 1/3 of that time on math, 25% of that time on science, 1/6 of that time on English, and 1/5 of that time on history. Calculate the maximum amount of time Alex can dedicate to foreign languages while still keeping his promise.\n\nTo determine the maximum amount of time Alex can dedicate to foreign languages while still spending less than 6 hours studying this week, let's first define the total time he committed to studying as \\( T \\) hours.\n\nAccording to the problem, Alex has already spent \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of \\( T \\) hours on math, \\(25\\%\\) of \\( T \\) hours on science, \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) of \\( T \\) hours on English, and \\(\\frac{1}{5} \\) of \\( T \\) hours on history. Let's calculate the time spent on each subject.\n\n1. Time spent on math: \\(\\frac{1}{3}T\\)\n2. Time spent on science: \\(0.25T = \\frac{1}{4}T\\)\n3. Time spent on English: \\(\\frac{1}{6}T\\)\n4. Time spent on history: \\(\\frac{1}{5}T\\)\n\nThe total time spent on these four subjects is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3}T + \\frac{1}{4}T + \\frac{1}{6}T + \\frac{1}{5}T\n\\]\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 5 is 60. So we convert each fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3}T = \\frac{20}{60}T, \\quad \\frac{1}{4}T = \\frac{15}{60}T, \\quad \\